WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.440
On February 14th, 1990, the Voyager 1 spacecraft was four billion miles from Earth.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.612
Almost six light-hours away.

00:00:08.612 --> 00:00:12.181
Its mission to the outer planets was complete.

00:00:12.181 --> 00:00:17.767
It was on its way out of the solar system, and it would never come back.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.715
The team at NASA sent a final command.

00:00:20.715 --> 00:00:21.646
Turn around.

00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:24.206
Look back at where you came from.

00:00:24.206 --> 00:00:25.603
Take a photograph.

00:00:25.603 --> 00:00:37.551
The image came back as a single point of light against the empty dark — less than one pixel across — suspended in a sunbeam the camera caught by accident.

00:00:37.551 --> 00:00:38.948
That dot is Earth.

00:00:38.948 --> 00:00:40.965
Carl Sagan wrote about it.

00:00:40.965 --> 00:00:57.025
Everyone you've ever heard of, he wrote — every saint and sinner, every founder of a religion, every couple in love, every parent waving goodbye to a child going to war — lived their entire life on that dot.

00:00:57.025 --> 00:00:59.896
Every aggregate of joy and suffering.

00:00:59.896 --> 00:01:01.835
Every confident religion.

00:01:01.835 --> 00:01:03.852
Every emperor and peasant.

00:01:03.852 --> 00:01:05.016
Happened there.

00:01:05.016 --> 00:01:07.033
You are looking at it now.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:09.982
Not the photograph — but the same dot.

00:01:09.982 --> 00:01:20.688
In this orrery you can pull back, see Earth's orbit around the Sun, see the Sun's place in the system, see the system's place in the dark.

00:01:20.688 --> 00:01:23.636
The view is the same view Voyager had.

00:01:23.636 --> 00:01:25.654
Only the distance differs.

00:01:25.654 --> 00:01:26.740
Take the tour.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.059
See where we are.

00:01:28.059 --> 00:01:30.386
See what we've done from here.

